## Decision and scope

Replace subject-only merging for date reports with evidence of the same cause. This record proposes replacing [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject); the accepted original remains unchanged as history. The old [triage rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) still describes the desk's current process until the desk accepts this replacement. Do not silently treat this proposal as deployed.

The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix. Its own computed views contradict that summary. Split the parser and booking-form hint work into separate engineering tickets. Keep a linked investigation checklist for reports whose bodies do not fit the computed classification.

## Counts from the export

<InvestigationSummary />

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What the queue shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart uses Monday-to-Sunday weeks and distinct report IDs. Received and merged are different events. A fall in merges cannot establish a fall in incidents; keep tracking new reports and verify a fix against newly received reports.

## Rule the desk can use while it keeps working

1. Read the report body. Look up the tenant's locale and form version in the tenant directory, and record the channel. Ask for missing facts rather than merging an unknown sender.
2. A month/day swap through app or email, or a booking-v2 form, goes to the parser ticket for a day-first tenant. A booking-v3 form whose MM/DD/YYYY hint conflicts with the tenant's day-first reader goes to the hint ticket. Confirm the described behavior agrees with the proposed route.
3. Merge only when the affected path and failure mechanism match. Subject text can help search; it cannot decide whether two reports share a cause. The same subject can hide two bugs, and different subjects can describe one bug.
4. Hold inconsistent or incomplete reports for investigation. In particular, a one-day move is not explained merely by a month/day classifier label. Keep the original report ID and link its investigation so it cannot vanish inside a larger ticket.
5. Reconcile counts by unique report ID. Preserve raw intake tags and merge history as evidence, but report them separately from verified causes.

## Check the rule against the running examples

<DedupeExplorer />

The same tenant can have different causes across channels; the same form channel can have different causes across tenants. Different subjects can share a cause. An unknown tenant does not establish a common cause. The classifier supports this check but does not read the body and cannot replace that step.

## Migration and verification

Once the desk accepts this decision, replace the live triage instructions, link both engineering tickets from #4471, and route new reports using the new rule immediately. Refile existing reports with an auditable ID-to-ticket mapping; preserve the original merge history. Update the incident summary to show both problems and the unresolved evidence. Do not rewrite accepted decision 0001.

For the parser fix, reproduce a day-first input such as 07/04/2026 and verify 7 April survives parsing and invite generation, rather than becoming 4 July. For the hint fix, verify booking-v3 displays the tenant's actual accepted order and saves the displayed date consistently. Verify both paths independently; one fix is not evidence for the other. Investigate the exceptional reports before calling the incident resolved.

The export was made on 2026-10-04. This review does not establish what happened after that date. The KB index and shared layout compute staleness: the old rule's review date is historical evidence, not proof its advice is still sound. Re-read the computed views whenever the data changes, and give this decision a fresh review when the desk accepts it.

